---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Mechanics - Replenish Guide | Premodern MTG"
    image="/src/assets/banners/seal.jpeg"
    description="Learn the complex rules interactions of the Replenish deck. Master the timing of Parallax Wave, Opalescence triggers, and how to permanently exile your opponent’s creatures."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Deck Mechanics"
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/seal.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Seal of Cleansing"
        imageCaption="Seal of Cleansing by Christopher Moeller"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="The Opal-Wave Permanent Exile Engine" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="mt-0">
            With <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                >Opalescence</a
            > and <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                >Parallax Wave</a
            > both out, Wave is a 4/4 that can target itself. Hold priority and stack
            up several exile activations on your opponent's creatures, then spend Wave's
            last fading counter targeting Wave itself. Wave leaves and comes right back
            as a brand-new object with five fresh fading counters. Now the earlier exile
            activations resolve—and since the Wave that would have returned those
            creatures is gone, they're exiled for good. Repeat until their board is
            empty. For the full stack breakdown, timestamps, and video walkthroughs of
            the Opal-Wave loop, check our <a
                href="/resources"
                class="guide-link"
                >External Resources</a
            > chapter.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Exiling Opposing Enchantments</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
            Because <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
            > animates <em>all</em> non-Aura enchantments on the battlefield, your
            opponent's enchantments become creatures too—and creatures are targetable.
            So you can use the same Wave loop to permanently exile problem permanents
            like <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Survival of the Fittest"
                >Survival of the Fittest</a
            >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sylvan Library"
                >Sylvan Library</a
            >, or an opposing <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            >.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Combat Sequencing & Summoning Sickness" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 class="mt-0">Parallax Wave Combat Sequencing</h3>
        <p>
            A mechanical consequence of the permanent exile loop is that <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > comes back as a new permanent, so it has summoning sickness and can't
            attack that turn. So when you can, temporarily exile their blockers before
            combat, swing with the whole team (including Wave for 4), and run the
            permanent-exile loop in your second main phase. The exception is creatures
            with nasty enter-the-battlefield triggers (like <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Goblin Ringleader">Goblin Ringleader</a
            > or <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Bone Shredder"
                >Bone Shredder</a
            >): temporary exile lets them come back and re-trigger, so exile those
            permanently before combat instead.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Opalescence & Post-Replenish Sickness</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
            A common mistake for new pilots is thinking a resolved <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
            > means you can swing for lethal that turn. <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
            > turns the returned enchantments into creatures, but anything that entered
            this turn has summoning sickness and can't attack. So you usually need to
            lock the board down first with <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > and <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
            >, keep yourself safe for a full turn, and swing for the kill after.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Decree of Silence Lock" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="m-0">
            When <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
            > is about to be sacrificed after its third counter, respond by activating
            an animated <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > targeting the Decree. Run the standard flicker loop and Decree comes back
            as a fresh object, ready to counter three more spells. There's a tiny window
            on the stack during the swap, but to exploit it the opponent would need to
            have cast three separate spells just to force the sacrifice and still have
            instant-speed interaction left over—a tall order for most Premodern decks.
            And even if they resolve removal on Opalescence in that gap, Decree just
            returns as a plain, non-creature enchantment, still ready to counter their
            next three plays.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Board Protection & Stack Interactions" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="mt-0">
            How you protect your board against a sweeper depends on whether it
            hits creatures or enchantments:
        </p>
        <ul class="pl-md mb-0">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Creature Sweepers (Wrath of God):</strong> Against a
                creature wipe like <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Wrath of God">Wrath of God</a
                >, one <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > is enough. It doesn't animate itself, so the only things Wrath
                can hit are your animated enchantments. Hold priority and use <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > to exile your own team; when the sweeper resolves and kills Wave,
                your exiled permanents come back untouched—and so does Opalescence.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-0">
                <strong>Enchantment Sweepers (Tranquil Domain):</strong>
                To survive a mass enchantment sweeper like <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tranquil Domain">Tranquil Domain</a
                >, you need <strong>two copies</strong> of <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                >. With only one, exiling it with Wave to save it backfires—the
                moment Opalescence leaves, Wave loses its creature status, so you
                can't keep looping or protect the rest of your board before
                Tranquil Domain wipes everything. With two Opalescence out, you
                can exile one and the second keeps Wave animated through the
                flicker. Since setting up two Opalescence just to dodge a sweeper
                is a lot to ask, the better plan against green decks with Tranquil
                Domain is to lock the spell out with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                > rather than rely on reactive Wave loops.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Stack Trick: Wave + Disenchant Exile</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
            You don't even need Opalescence to exile creatures permanently—<a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            > or a sideboard <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
            > works too. Activate <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > targeting up to five of their creatures. With those activations still on
            the stack, crack your Seal (or cast Disenchant) to destroy your own Wave.
            Wave leaves before it exiles anything, so its leaves-the-battlefield clause
            returns nothing. Then the exile activations resolve and their creatures are
            gone for good—a huge defensive play in the early turns.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Attunement's Activation Cost Immunity</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
            The activation syntax of <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
            > reads: "Return Attunement to its owner's hand: Draw three cards, then
            discard four cards." Bouncing it to your hand is part of the <em>cost</em>,
            not an effect on the stack, so you can activate it in response to removal
            like <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
            > or an opposing <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            >. Attunement is back in your hand before their spell resolves—their removal
            fizzles and you still get the full dig.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Post-Sideboard Creature Synergies" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="mt-0">
            Beyond clearing adversarial boards, <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > exhibits profound synergy with your own creature packages, particularly
            post-sideboard. <em
                >Note: These maneuvers rely on active <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > and Wave presence to execute immediate loop flickers.</em
            >
        </p>
        <ul class="pl-md mb-0">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Flipping Exalted Angel for Free:</strong> Deploying <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                > face-down via morph for three colorless mana permits targeting it
                with <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >. Executing an Opal-Wave flicker immediately returns the Angel
                to the battlefield face-up, entirely circumventing the 2WW
                unmorph cost while presenting an immediate 4/5 flying lifelink
                threat.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-0">
                <strong>Resetting Meddling Mage:</strong> If an opponent reveals alternative
                threat avenues or circumvents your primary named restriction, target
                your own <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > with Wave before initiating an immediate flicker loop. Upon re-entering,
                Mage permits declaring an entirely new target name tailored to the
                evolving board state.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Mana Mechanics & Tax Evasion" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 class="mt-0">Asymmetrical Tsabo's Web</h3>
        <p>
            Competitive Replenish relies on <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
            > to paralyze disruptive opposing lands including <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
            >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wasteland"
                >Wasteland</a
            >, and <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Dust Bowl"
                >Dust Bowl</a
            >. This restriction remains completely asymmetrical because Tsabo's
            Web strictly targets lands possessing activated abilities that <em
                >do not</em
            > generate mana. All of our non-basic filtering lands
            (<a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Skycloud Expanse"
                >Skycloud Expanse</a
            >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Adarkar Wastes"
                >Adarkar Wastes</a
            >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Ancient Tomb"
                >Ancient Tomb</a
            >) exclusively carry mana-generating abilities, untapping
            effortlessly under the lock.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Frantic Search Mana Tricks</h3>
        <p>
            On top of being a cheap way to churn through your deck, the untap clause
            within <a
                role="button" tabindex="0"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
            > unlocks pivotal logistical combat mana tricks:
        </p>
        <ul class="pl-md mb-0">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Netting Mana via Ancient Tomb:</strong> While Frantic Search
                conventionally operates as a zero-net-cost discard mechanism, resolving
                it when untapping <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
                > actively ramps mana production. For example, tapping three lands
                (including Tomb) floats 4 mana; spending 3 on <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > leaves 1 mana floating before untapping those exact three lands.
                You now command 5 available mana on turn three—enabling an immediate
                <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                > while preserving open interaction mana.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-0">
                <strong>Bypassing Upkeep Tap Disruptors:</strong> When confronting
                opposing upkeep mana denial such as <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                > activations or <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tangle Wire">Tangle Wire</a
                > tap triggers, float all available mana across your targeted lands
                in response. After the tap effects resolve and exhaust your board state,
                immediately use that floating mana to cast <a
                    role="button" tabindex="0"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >—cleanly untapping three core lands to execute main-phase development
                and completely neutralize their interactive tempo play.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>
</Layout>
